# ── Ingot ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every value here is the default the code already uses, written out so that
# the shape of the configuration is readable in one place. Copy to .env and
# change what you need.
#
# Three things have no default and refuse to boot without one: the database,
# the base tier, and — below — who is allowed to call this service.

# ── Authentication ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Who may call this service. There is no default, and unlike the model
# selectors there is no harmless stand-in to fall back to: a service that
# guessed this would be guessing who may read the memories in it.
#
#   sealed   one account and one root key, both from this file. Nothing to
#            sign up for, nothing to sign into, and no third party in the
#            authentication path. This is the whole self-hosting story.
INGOT_AUTH=sealed

# ── sealed ── the account, and the key that opens it.
#
# The account is created at boot if it is not there, so this is the only place
# it has to be named. The slug is the first segment of every route: with the
# value below, a memory lives at /api/v1/dev/:ingot.
INGOT_ACCOUNT=dev
# INGOT_ACCOUNT_NAME=A development account

# The root key. GENERATE THIS — do not think one up:
#
#   echo "ing_sk_$(openssl rand -base64 24 | tr '+/' '-_' | tr -d '=')"
#
# It is the one credential that cannot be revoked from inside the service, so
# rotating it is a change here and a restart. Keys minted at runtime through
# POST /:account/keys still work and are still revocable — this is the key that
# can never be locked out, not the only one that exists.
#
# Anything shorter than 31 characters is refused at boot, as is anything not
# starting with `ing_sk_`. The value below is a real generated key and is fine
# for a laptop; it is in a file in a public repository, so it is not fine
# anywhere else.
INGOT_API_KEY=ing_sk_lTSHqlj8rrBs0vo44ehULQPVshsucTXB

# Every variable above can also be given as <NAME>_FILE pointing at a mounted
# file, which is how a Kubernetes Secret or a Docker secret gets a credential
# into the process without it appearing in /proc/<pid>/environ or a crash dump.
# INGOT_API_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/ingot-api-key

# The service itself.
PORT=3002
CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5174

# Postgres holds the catalogue and the overlay — never the Parquet.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://ingot:ingot@localhost:5432/ingot
DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# The suite's database. Truncated between assertions, so keep it separate from
# the one you develop against.
INGOT_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://ingot:ingot@localhost:5432/ingot_test

# ── The base tier ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where Parquet goes. The one decision nobody can make on your behalf, so it is
# named rather than inferred: a missing or half-filled configuration refuses to
# boot instead of quietly writing to local disk. Setting a bucket variable
# without naming the driver is refused for the same reason.
#
#   filesystem   a local path. Not a stub — a single node with a volume, or a
#                bucket mounted by something else (a GCS FUSE CSI volume, an
#                S3 CSI driver), is a perfectly good way to run this.
#   s3           AWS S3 and anything speaking its protocol: MinIO, R2, Ceph.
#   gcs          Google Cloud Storage, held by a service account.
INGOT_STORAGE=filesystem

# ── filesystem ──
INGOT_DATA_DIR=.ingot-data

# ── s3 ── all three are required. MinIO, as docker-compose.yml runs it:
# INGOT_STORAGE=s3
# INGOT_S3_BUCKET=ingot
# INGOT_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=ingot
# INGOT_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=ingotingot
# INGOT_S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
# INGOT_S3_REGION=us-east-1
# Path-style addressing, which MinIO and most gateways need and AWS does not.
# Defaults to true whenever an endpoint is set.
# INGOT_S3_PATH_STYLE=true

# ── gcs ── the bucket, and nothing else.
#
# There is no key to configure because the credential is not ours to hold:
# Application Default Credentials find it — the metadata server under a GKE
# workload identity, GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS pointing at a mounted key
# file, or `gcloud auth application-default login` on a laptop. The service
# account needs read and write on the bucket (roles/storage.objectAdmin, or
# objectViewer plus objectCreator and objectUser).
#
# Worth knowing before you deploy it: DuckDB cannot *write* to GCS with a
# service account — its gs:// support takes an HMAC interoperability key and
# nothing else. So reads go through DuckDB over https with a short-lived token,
# and a roll-up copies Parquet to INGOT_STAGING_DIR and uploads it. That
# directory wants real space and wants to be a mounted volume; see
# GcsObjectStore for the whole story.
# INGOT_STORAGE=gcs
# INGOT_GCS_BUCKET=my-ingot-memories
# A stand-in for the real API. Only for testing against an emulator.
# INGOT_GCS_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4443

# Scratch space for uploads. Defaults to the system temp directory.
# INGOT_STAGING_DIR=/var/lib/ingot/staging

# ── The models ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two selectors, not one, because they are two purchases. Embedding is a
# per-row cost paid once; a summary is an LLM call paid every time a caller
# asks for `receipt: "full"`. A deployment should be able to have real
# semantic search without buying the second, so they are chosen separately
# from the same set: `local`, `openai`, `gcp`.
#
# Both default to `local` — deterministic offline stand-ins, so the suite and a
# laptop need no network, no key and no bill. They are good defaults and bad
# surprises, so each says at boot that it is one.
#
# A provider named without its credentials REFUSES TO BOOT, for the reason
# INGOT_STORAGE does. Falling back to the stand-in would leave a service that
# answers, accepts writes and hands back receipts that are lexical nonsense —
# with the only evidence a warning nobody was watching for.
INGOT_EMBEDDER=local
INGOT_SUMMARISER=local

# How long a model has to answer before the attempt is abandoned. One retry on
# a rate limit happens inside this; a longer wait is left to the next sweep.
# Bounded above at 150000, for the reason INGOT_DELIVERY_TIMEOUT_MS is: a batch
# is claimed under a five-minute lease and the model is asked with the
# transaction closed, so a call still running when that lease lapses is a batch
# a second replica may claim and pay for as well.
# INGOT_AI_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# ── openai ── the key, and anything else you want to override.
#
# OPENAI_BASE_URL is what makes an Azure deployment, a gateway, or a local vLLM
# this adapter rather than a fourth one — anything speaking the OpenAI API.
#
# The width is DECLARED, never discovered. It is baked into every stored vector
# and into the FLOAT[N] column a query session builds, so changing it is a
# re-embed rather than a configuration change. text-embedding-3-* honours it as
# a request parameter, and a model that returns a different width is refused
# rather than quietly writing vectors that every ranking then drops.
# INGOT_EMBEDDER=openai
# INGOT_SUMMARISER=openai
# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-…
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
# INGOT_OPENAI_EMBEDDING_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small
# INGOT_OPENAI_EMBEDDING_DIMENSIONS=1536
# INGOT_OPENAI_SUMMARY_MODEL=gpt-4.1-mini

# ── gcp ── Vertex AI. The project, and nothing else that is secret.
#
# There is no key here for the same reason the GCS driver has none: the
# credential is not ours to hold. Application Default Credentials find it — the
# metadata server under a GKE workload identity, GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS
# pointing at a mounted key file, or `gcloud auth application-default login`.
# The service account needs roles/aiplatform.user on the project below. The
# project is still required, because a token does not say whose quota to spend.
# INGOT_EMBEDDER=gcp
# INGOT_SUMMARISER=gcp
# INGOT_GCP_PROJECT=my-project
# INGOT_GCP_LOCATION=us-central1
# INGOT_GCP_EMBEDDING_MODEL=text-embedding-004
# INGOT_GCP_EMBEDDING_DIMENSIONS=768
# INGOT_GCP_SUMMARY_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash
# A stand-in for the real API. Only for testing against an emulator.
# INGOT_GCP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:8081

# ── Reading scans ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# What reads a page a PDF has no text layer for — a scan, a photocopy, a
# screenshot printed to PDF. A third purchase, and the only one that is off
# unless asked for: embedding and summarising have free stand-ins, and there is
# no cheap approximation of reading a photograph of a page.
#
# It is a FALLBACK, never a mode. A page reaches an engine only when the
# document's own text layer gave nothing for it, so a PDF that has text is read
# the way it always was, at no cost — and a scan stapled into a text export
# costs one page rather than the document.
#
#   off      a blank page stays blank. The default.
#   local    Tesseract, in this process. No network, no bill, ~350ms a page,
#            and it does not invent digits. Wants INGOT_TESSDATA_DIR.
#   openai   a vision model. Better on a bad scan; seconds and money a page,
#            and it can misread a figure with confidence.
#   gcp      the same, on Vertex.
#
# Every chunk read this way carries the engine that read it in its `ocr`
# column, so `WHERE ocr IS NULL` is the text a document actually contained.
# That column is the answer to "can I trust this figure": OCR text is
# machine-read and not extracted.
# INGOT_OCR=off

# Where <language>.traineddata lives.
#
# Required by INGOT_OCR=local, and it is also what puts Tesseract *behind* a
# model: name both and a page the model refuses or times out on is read
# in-process rather than lost, with the boot line saying so and the chunk
# naming the engine. Name a model without this and a refused page stays blank.
#
# It is required rather than defaulted because tesseract.js downloads its
# language data from a CDN when it is not given a path — a parse reaching the
# network on behalf of an uploaded document, which is the one thing no handler
# here does. The image bakes it; see apps/ingot/Dockerfile. Locally:
#   mkdir -p .tessdata && curl -fsSL -o .tessdata/eng.traineddata \
#     https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tesseract-ocr/tessdata_fast/4.1.0/eng.traineddata
# INGOT_TESSDATA_DIR=/opt/tessdata
# INGOT_OCR_LANGUAGE=eng

# Pages of one document an engine will be given, from page 1 down. A budget
# rather than a limit on what a scan can be: 300 pages through a hosted model
# is a bill nobody chose and a parse that outlives the claim it is held under.
# Pages past it stay blank.
# INGOT_OCR_MAX_PAGES=20
# Pages in flight at once, for a hosted model. Tesseract is always one.
# INGOT_OCR_CONCURRENCY=4
# INGOT_OPENAI_OCR_MODEL=gpt-4.1-mini
# INGOT_GCP_OCR_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash

# ── The query engine ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A query materialises the tables it names into an in-memory DuckDB, so these
# are the ceiling on what one caller's question can cost. `INGOT_MAX_TABLE_ROWS`
# is the one that produces a clear error rather than a slow one.
INGOT_QUERY_MEMORY_LIMIT=1GB
INGOT_QUERY_THREADS=2
INGOT_MAX_TABLE_ROWS=2000000

# Where DuckDB looks for httpfs. Point this at a directory baked into the image
# so a cold container is not fetching from extensions.duckdb.org on its first
# query — which is a first query that fails on a locked-down network.
# INGOT_DUCKDB_EXTENSION_DIR=/opt/duckdb-extensions

# ── Background work ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing to configure, and that is the point of the design rather than an
# omission. The roll-up, the embedding backlog, the receipt queue, receipt
# delivery and expiry are timers inside this process, each taking a Postgres
# advisory lock so that one replica sweeps at a time; /add wakes the embedding
# and receipt workers the moment it commits and a written receipt wakes
# delivery, so the timer is the floor rather than the only path. There is no
# second listener and no address to point at.

# How many drains of each queue run at once, per replica. This is the only
# thing between a burst of writes and an unbounded burst of calls at whatever
# INGOT_EMBEDDER and INGOT_SUMMARISER name — and it is *per replica*, because
# the wake path takes no advisory lock. What your provider sees is this times
# the pod count, and an autoscaler moves that on CPU. Set them against a quota
# divided by your replica ceiling, not against one pod.
# INGOT_EMBEDDINGS_CONCURRENCY=2
# INGOT_RECEIPTS_CONCURRENCY=2
# Documents parsed at once. The lowest of the four, and bounded by something
# different: the other three limit calls at somebody else's service, and this
# one limits OUR OWN HEAP. A parse holds the whole document in memory — a zip
# is read from its central directory and a PDF from its trailer, so neither
# streams — so the number to size a pod against is this times two passes times
# INGOT_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES times your replica count.
# INGOT_FILES_CONCURRENCY=2
# Higher out of the box: a delivery goes to a receiver the caller nominated, so
# six concurrent ones are six different endpoints rather than six calls at one
# provider, and one slow receiver must not hold up everybody else's.
# INGOT_DELIVERIES_CONCURRENCY=6

# ── Delivering receipts ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A receipt is collected by polling by default: /add hands back a SELECT and
# the caller runs it. A memory can also nominate somewhere to push each receipt
# as it lands — POST /:account/:ingot/config — and these decide what this
# deployment is willing to do about that.
#
# The split matters. A memory's owner chooses *where among their own things* a
# receipt goes: an endpoint, a queue name. The operator chooses what this
# service will connect to at all. A tenant naming a broker URL would be a
# tenant choosing where this service opens an authenticated connection, which
# is why that is here and not in the API.
#
# Webhooks need nothing set: they work out of the box, and endpoints pointing
# at loopback, link-local or private addresses are refused when a memory is
# configured. Leave INGOT_RABBITMQ_URL unset and { "t": "rmq" } is refused at
# that same moment, naming this variable — rather than accepted and then
# failing every delivery afterwards in a log the caller cannot see.
# INGOT_RABBITMQ_URL=amqps://user:pass@broker:5671
# The default exchange routes a message to the queue named by the routing key,
# which is exactly what a per-memory queue name is. Set this only if you want
# your own topology and will bind the queues yourself.
# INGOT_RABBITMQ_EXCHANGE=
# A receiver that has not answered in this long is not going to. Bounded above
# at 150000: a delivery is claimed under a five-minute lease and the call is
# made with the transaction closed, so a timeout past half that lease is a
# delivery still in flight when a second replica may claim it — at-least-once
# quietly becoming reliably-twice. Refused at boot rather than left to chance.
# INGOT_DELIVERY_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
# Attempts before a delivery is left alone. Higher than a receipt's four,
# because a receipt that fails four times is usually a body a model will not
# summarise — the same refusal, repeated — while a delivery that fails is
# usually somebody else's service being down, which is a thing that ends.
# INGOT_DELIVERY_ATTEMPTS=10
# INGOT_DELIVERY_USER_AGENT=ingot-receipts/1

# ── Documents ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# POST /:account/:ingot/file takes a PDF, a deck or a spreadsheet and turns it
# into two ordinary tables in the memory — chunks in `ingot_chunks`, and, when
# the caller asks for it, typed rows in a table of their own. Nothing here has
# to be set.
#
# The ceiling on one upload. It is HEAP rather than policy: the bytes are held
# whole to be parsed, because a zip is read from its central directory and a
# PDF from its trailer, and neither streams. So this times
# INGOT_FILES_CONCURRENCY times your replica count is memory somebody has to
# have. Raising it a long way wants a look at INGOT_QUERY_MEMORY_LIMIT in the
# same breath — the two draw on the same heap.
# INGOT_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=33554432
#
# How much text goes in one chunk, and how much of the previous one to repeat.
#
# Note what is NOT configurable: WHICH boundary a document is split on. That is
# decided by what the document is — a slide is one chunk, a Word section is one
# chunk, a PDF page is one chunk, and only plain text gets a token window —
# because there is no case where cutting a deck every 512 tokens beats cutting
# it every slide. HOW MUCH is a function of your embedder's window and of what
# you intend to put back into a model's context, and this service knows
# neither, so it is yours. Both can also be set per upload.
#
# Overlap is only ever applied where the boundary was OURS: a slide does not
# bleed into the next slide, so nothing is repeated across one.
# INGOT_CHUNK_TOKENS=512
# INGOT_CHUNK_OVERLAP_TOKENS=64

# ── Observability ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# /metrics is on its own listener, not reachable through the public ingress.
METRICS_PORT=9465
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318
